1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:03,977
Every few days, a photo like this lands in the street group chat.

2
00:00:04,327 --> 00:00:05,756
Tomorrow's power cuts.

3
00:00:05,756 --> 00:00:08,778
And everyone does the same sum in their head.

4
00:00:09,128 --> 00:00:10,527
When is my area off?

5
00:00:10,527 --> 00:00:12,725
And what can I still get done?

6
00:00:13,125 --> 00:00:14,196
This is OnHours.

7
00:00:14,196 --> 00:00:17,675
Take a photo of the notice, and it reads every area,

8
00:00:17,675 --> 00:00:19,113
with its power cuts.

9
00:00:19,513 --> 00:00:20,365
Choose yours.

10
00:00:20,365 --> 00:00:23,511
Keep your jobs, or type new ones in plain words.

11
00:00:23,511 --> 00:00:24,791
Then plan the day.

12
00:00:25,691 --> 00:00:29,081
Every job gets one unbroken stretch when the power is on.

13
00:00:29,081 --> 00:00:32,334
Nothing starts, and then loses power halfway through.

14
00:00:32,734 --> 00:00:34,824
The AI only reads the notice.

15
00:00:34,824 --> 00:00:37,419
Plain, tested code places every job,

16
00:00:37,419 --> 00:00:40,834
so the same notice always gives the same plan.

17
00:00:41,234 --> 00:00:43,400
It copes with a messy picture too.

18
00:00:43,400 --> 00:00:47,987
This one is tilted, laid out differently, and writes the times in words.

19
00:00:47,987 --> 00:00:52,419
It still reads five areas, and splits the overnight cut at midnight.

20
00:00:53,719 --> 00:00:56,413
When a job can't fit, OnHours says why.

21
00:00:56,413 --> 00:00:59,038
These beans need five hours in one go,

22
00:00:59,038 --> 00:01:02,661
and the longest stretch left is four hours fifteen.

23
00:01:03,461 --> 00:01:06,213
If you have an inverter, say how long it runs.

24
00:01:06,213 --> 00:01:09,622
Now the beans fit, and the plan tells you when to switch:

25
00:01:09,622 --> 00:01:12,534
eight o'clock, for the last forty-five minutes.

26
00:01:12,934 --> 00:01:16,128
Backup is just one number: the hours it runs.

27
00:01:16,128 --> 00:01:17,719
Nothing to calculate.

28
00:01:18,319 --> 00:01:20,745
Then the chat sends a revised notice.

29
00:01:20,745 --> 00:01:23,891
Paste it, and OnHours shows what moved, and why.

30
00:01:23,891 --> 00:01:27,990
The iron shifts to four o'clock, and the beans no longer fit.

31
00:01:29,690 --> 00:01:32,322
And here is the part that matters when the lights go out.

32
00:01:32,722 --> 00:01:34,423
OnHours installs on the phone.

33
00:01:34,423 --> 00:01:38,207
Next time you open it, your jobs and your plan are already there.

34
00:01:39,507 --> 00:01:41,746
Switch on airplane mode, and reload.

35
00:01:41,746 --> 00:01:46,075
It still tells you the power is off until nine, and what comes next.

36
00:01:47,975 --> 00:01:50,409
Your jobs and your last notice stay on the phone.

37
00:01:50,409 --> 00:01:52,694
There is no account, and nothing is tracked.

38
00:01:54,194 --> 00:01:56,848
Send the plan to the family chat as a picture,

39
00:01:56,848 --> 00:01:58,405
or add it to your calendar,

40
00:01:58,405 --> 00:02:01,909
with a reminder ten minutes before every job and every cut.

41
00:02:03,309 --> 00:02:06,723
It is for anyone who runs a home, or a small business,

42
00:02:06,723 --> 00:02:08,493
around scheduled power cuts.

43
00:02:08,493 --> 00:02:11,880
The tailor, the baker, the family with a water pump.

44
00:02:12,280 --> 00:02:14,668
Wherever the schedule arrives as a message,

45
00:02:14,668 --> 00:02:17,878
OnHours turns it into a day someone can actually follow.

46
00:02:18,378 --> 00:02:22,405
We built it plan first, with the Devpost skill pack: scope,

47
00:02:22,405 --> 00:02:24,452
requirements, spec, then code.

48
00:02:24,452 --> 00:02:27,318
It taught us to give the AI one small job,

49
00:02:27,318 --> 00:02:30,694
and to keep every decision in code you can test.

50
00:02:31,594 --> 00:02:32,153
OnHours.

51
00:02:32,153 --> 00:02:34,767
Plan your day around the power cuts.

52
00:02:34,967 --> 00:02:39,067
Open source on GitHub: psychofict/onhours
